# head-toto — Project Overview

## What This Project Is

`head-toto` is a faithful CLI replica of the GNU Core Utilities `head` command.
It copies the **first part of each input** to stdout — by default the first
**10 lines** of each FILE — then exits. With no FILE, or when FILE is `-`, it
reads standard input. It does not loop or follow files.

Input selection and counting:

| Mode | Option | Unit counted |
|------|--------|--------------|
| Lines (default) | `-n NUM` / `--lines=NUM` | lines terminated by `\n` (or `\0` with `-z`) |
| Bytes | `-c NUM` / `--bytes=NUM` | raw bytes |

When more than one FILE is given, each is preceded by a header
`==> NAME <==` (stdin is shown as `standard input`), with a blank line
between consecutive files.

## Binary Name

The compiled binary must always be named **`head-toto`**. Never rename it.
It is produced at **`build/head-toto`** (MinGW may also emit
`build/head-toto.exe`; that is the same program). The unit-test binary is
**`build/tests/test_core`**.

## Platforms

| Platform | Build environment | Notes |
|----------|-------------------|-------|
| **Linux / WSL** | `gcc`/`clang` + GNU Make | POSIX `open`/`read`/`write`; full unit tests |
| **Windows** | **MSYS2 UCRT64** or **Git Bash** + UCRT64 `gcc` on `PATH` | Makefile auto-selects binary-mode I/O when the `_O_BINARY` probe succeeds |
| **Git Bash** | Put `/c/msys64/ucrt64/bin` **before** other toolchains on `PATH` | Avoid minimal MinGW toolchains; see `toolchain-structure.mdc` |

Binaries are **not portable** across operating systems — build separately on Linux and on Windows.

## Scope — Keep It Minimal

Supporting behaviour:

- Built-in `--help` / `--h` and `--version` / `--v` (detected anywhere in `argv`
  before `--`; help wins if both appear)
- `-n NUM`, `-nNUM`, `--lines=NUM`, `--lines NUM` → first NUM lines (default 10)
- `-c NUM`, `-cNUM`, `--bytes=NUM`, `--bytes NUM` → first NUM bytes
- `-q` / `--quiet` / `--silent` → never print headers
- `-v` / `--verbose` → always print headers
- `-z` / `--zero-terminated` → line delimiter is NUL, not newline
- Short options may be clustered (`-qz`, `-qn5`); `--` ends option parsing
- Options and FILE operands may be interleaved (`head-toto a.txt -n 3`)
- FILE `-` (or no FILE) → standard input
- Last `-n`/`-c` wins; last `-q`/`-v` wins
- NUM is plain unsigned decimal; values beyond `UINTMAX_MAX` clamp to `UINTMAX_MAX`
- Unreadable FILE → diagnostic on stderr, continue with remaining FILEs, exit 1
- CLI flag strings live in `head_toto_cli.h` (`HEAD_TOTO_ARG_*`); `scan_meta_flags()`
  and the option parser use `argv_has_exact()` / macro comparisons only

**Do not add:**

- Interactive or TUI modes
- Follow / tail-like behaviour
- Negative "all but the last" counts (`-n -K`, `-c -K`) — rejected as invalid numbers
- Multiplier suffixes on NUM (`K`, `M`, `b`, `KiB`, …) — rejected as invalid numbers
- Obsolete `-NUM` syntax (`head-toto -5`) — rejected as an invalid option
- Logging or debug output in normal operation
- Single-dash `-h` as help (only `--help`, `--h`, `--version`, and `--v` are meta flags;
  `-v` is **verbose**, as in GNU `head`)
- Any feature that would break GNU `head` behavioural compatibility for the supported options

## Behavioural Contract

| Invocation | Output | Exit |
|---|---|---|
| `head-toto FILE` | first 10 lines of FILE, no header | `0` |
| `head-toto -n 3 FILE` | first 3 lines of FILE | `0` |
| `head-toto -c 20 FILE` | first 20 bytes of FILE | `0` |
| `head-toto -n 0 FILE` | nothing (FILE is still opened) | `0` |
| `head-toto A B` | `==> A <==\n…\n==> B <==\n…` | `0` |
| `head-toto -q A B` | contents of A then B, no headers | `0` |
| `head-toto -v A` | `==> A <==\n…` | `0` |
| `head-toto` / `head-toto -` | first 10 lines of stdin | `0` |
| `head-toto --version` / `--v` | prints `head-toto <version>\n` | `0` |
| `head-toto --help` / `--h` | prints usage on stdout | `0` |
| `head-toto missing A` | stderr: `head-toto: cannot open 'missing' for reading: <reason>`; A still printed | `1` |
| `head-toto DIR` | stderr: `head-toto: error reading 'DIR': <reason>` | `1` |
| `head-toto -n abc` | stderr: `head-toto: invalid number of lines: 'abc'` | `1` |
| `head-toto -c abc` | stderr: `head-toto: invalid number of bytes: 'abc'` | `1` |
| `head-toto -x` | stderr: `head-toto: invalid option -- 'x'` + hint | `1` |
| `head-toto --foo` | stderr: `head-toto: unrecognized option '--foo'` + hint | `1` |
| `head-toto -n` | stderr: `head-toto: option requires an argument -- 'n'` + hint | `1` |
| Write failure | stderr: `head-toto: write: <reason>` | `1` |

`--help` anywhere in `argv` (before `--`) wins over `--version`.
Usage errors are detected before any input is read or output is written.

## Exit Codes

| Code | Meaning |
|------|---------|
| `0` | Success — every FILE copied (or `--help` / `--version`) |
| `1` | Error — usage error, any FILE failed to open/read, or write failure |

